import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.DaD4z1Q-.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"How it works","description":"How Strauss-RS prefixes a Composer project: the stages of a run, the PHP parser and lexer it reads code with, and the Rust crates used for parallelism, globbing, patterns and output.","frontmatter":{"title":"How it works","description":"How Strauss-RS prefixes a Composer project: the stages of a run, the PHP parser and lexer it reads code with, and the Rust crates used for parallelism, globbing, patterns and output."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"how-it-works.md","filePath":"how-it-works.md"}`),a={name:`how-it-works.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t('<h1 id="how-it-works" tabindex="-1">How it works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink to “How it works”">​</a></h1><p>This page is for people who want to know what happens between <code>strauss-rs prefix</code> and the files that come out, and which Rust crates do the work. You don&#39;t need any of it to use Strauss-RS.</p><p>The short version: every PHP file is parsed into a syntax tree, the names it declares and uses are collected, a plan says what each name becomes, and the plan is applied as exact byte-range edits to the original text. The source is never printed back from a tree, so formatting and comments survive untouched.</p><h2 id="the-stages" tabindex="-1">The stages <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-stages" aria-label="Permalink to “The stages”">​</a></h2><p><code>prefix</code> runs these in order. Each stage reads what the one before produced and none of them writes anything until the last.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Stage</th><th style="text-align:left;">Module</th><th style="text-align:left;">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Configuration</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>config</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Reads <code>strauss.json</code> and <code>composer.json</code>, fills in defaults, infers prefixes and validates every setting.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Packages</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>packages</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Finds the packages through <code>installed.json</code> and lists their files.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Symbol scan</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>symbols</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Parses every PHP file and collects the symbols each package declares.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Rename plan</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>rename</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Decides which symbols are renamed, and to what.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Edit plan</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>edits</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Works out every change needed in the files so the renamed symbols still resolve.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Output</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>output</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Copies the files with their edits made, writes the autoloader, updates your own files and cleans up.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>plan</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>symbols</code>, <code>files</code>, <code>packages</code> and <code>config</code> stop partway and print what they have. That is why they are safe to run at any time: they share the stages above and only <code>prefix</code> reaches the output stage. <code>--dry-run</code> runs the output stage without writing.</p><h2 id="reading-php" tabindex="-1">Reading PHP <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-php" aria-label="Permalink to “Reading PHP”">​</a></h2><p>PHP can&#39;t be prefixed with search and replace. <code>Client</code> might be a class in your namespace, an imported name, a string in a docblock or a method called <code>Client</code>. Strauss-RS parses the code so it knows which.</p><h3 id="php-rs-parser-php-ast-and-php-lexer" tabindex="-1"><code>php-rs-parser</code>, <code>php-ast</code> and <code>php-lexer</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#php-rs-parser-php-ast-and-php-lexer" aria-label="Permalink to “php-rs-parser, php-ast and php-lexer”">​</a></h3><p>These three crates are the PHP front end.</p><ul><li><strong><code>php-rs-parser</code></strong> turns source into a syntax tree. A file that doesn&#39;t parse is reported and skipped; the other files carry on.</li><li><strong><code>php-ast</code></strong> defines that tree, including the <code>owned</code> form the analyser works with, and a <code>NameResolver</code>. PHP names are resolved against the surrounding <code>namespace</code> and <code>use</code> statements, so <code>new Client</code> becomes <code>GuzzleHttp\\Client</code>, and an unqualified function call is treated as the namespaced function or the global one, as PHP does.</li><li><strong><code>php-lexer</code></strong> produces the token stream.</li></ul><p>Why both a tree and tokens? The tree has the structure but not every position. It doesn&#39;t give the position of the name in <code>class Foo</code>, and for an item inside a group <code>use A\\{B, C}</code> the position it gives runs from the start of the statement. The analyser (<code>analyser/spans.rs</code>) finds those exact locations from the tokens. Strings and comments also come from the tokens (<code>analyser/regions.rs</code>), because a string like <code>&#39;Acme\\\\Lib\\\\Client&#39;</code> or a <code>@param</code> in a docblock can name a symbol without being code the parser resolves.</p><p>The result for each file is a list of declarations (classes, interfaces, traits, enums, functions and constants) and references, each with a byte span.</p><h2 id="doing-it-in-parallel" tabindex="-1">Doing it in parallel <a class="header-anchor" href="#doing-it-in-parallel" aria-label="Permalink to “Doing it in parallel”">​</a></h2><h3 id="rayon" tabindex="-1"><code>rayon</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rayon" aria-label="Permalink to “rayon”">​</a></h3><p>Parsing is the expensive part, and it is independent for each file, so it is spread over a <code>rayon</code> thread pool (<code>parallel.rs</code>). Rayon hands files to workers as they become free rather than splitting the list up front, so one huge file doesn&#39;t leave the other workers idle. The same pool is used to list package files, plan edits and write output.</p><p>Things worth knowing:</p><ul><li>Workers are threads of one process, unlike PHP Strauss where each worker is a process. The default <code>parallel</code> setting uses every CPU.</li><li>The analyser recurses into nested code, so each worker gets a 16 MiB stack, and even a single worker runs inside the pool rather than on the smaller main thread stack.</li><li>Results come back in input order, so the output does not depend on the number of workers. This is covered by tests that run with different worker counts.</li><li>Analysis runs inside <code>catch_unwind</code>. A bug that a particular file triggers is reported against that file and doesn&#39;t stop the run.</li></ul><h2 id="finding-packages-and-files" tabindex="-1">Finding packages and files <a class="header-anchor" href="#finding-packages-and-files" aria-label="Permalink to “Finding packages and files”">​</a></h2><h3 id="serde-and-serde-json" tabindex="-1"><code>serde</code> and <code>serde_json</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#serde-and-serde-json" aria-label="Permalink to “serde and serde_json”">​</a></h3><p><code>installed.json</code>, <code>composer.json</code> and <code>strauss.json</code> are read with <code>serde</code>. Settings are first read into a raw, permissive shape (<code>config/raw.rs</code>) and then validated into the <code>Config</code> the rest of the program uses, so every error message can name the setting at fault. <code>serde_json</code> is built with <code>preserve_order</code>, so JSON that Strauss-RS writes, such as <code>init</code> and <code>migrate</code> output, keeps the order of keys the user already had.</p><p>The <code>--json</code> output of <code>config</code>, <code>packages</code>, <code>files</code>, <code>symbols</code>, <code>plan</code> and <code>prefix</code> is these same types serialised.</p><h3 id="ignore" tabindex="-1"><code>ignore</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#ignore" aria-label="Permalink to “ignore”">​</a></h3><p>Package directories and your own source are walked with <code>ignore::WalkBuilder</code>. It is configured to read only what&#39;s asked of it: hidden files are included, parent and global ignore rules are off, and links aren&#39;t followed. For a package that opts in, <code>.gitignore</code> rules are honoured. The same walker finds your own files for <code>update_call_sites</code>.</p><h3 id="globset" tabindex="-1"><code>globset</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#globset" aria-label="Permalink to “globset”">​</a></h3><p>A package&#39;s <code>.gitattributes</code> marks files it leaves out of its release archive with <code>export-ignore</code>: tests, CI configuration and so on. Strauss-RS reads those lines, compiles each pattern with <code>globset</code> and leaves the matching files out of the copy. A pattern without a slash matches a name in any directory, as it does in git; one with a slash matches from the package root.</p><h3 id="fancy-regex" tabindex="-1"><code>fancy-regex</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#fancy-regex" aria-label="Permalink to “fancy-regex”">​</a></h3><p>Patterns in <code>strauss.json</code>, such as <code>file_patterns</code> and <code>namespace_replacement_patterns</code>, were written for PHP&#39;s <code>preg_match()</code> and <code>preg_replace()</code>, so they have to keep working. <code>fancy-regex</code> is used instead of <code>regex</code> because PCRE patterns commonly use lookarounds and backreferences, which <code>regex</code> doesn&#39;t support. <code>php_pattern.rs</code> wraps it to add what PHP does around it: optional delimiters (<code>~foo~</code>), literal-or-regex keys, and <code>preg_replace</code> replacement syntax (<code>$1</code>, <code>${1}</code>, <code>\\1</code>).</p><h2 id="deciding-what-to-rename" tabindex="-1">Deciding what to rename <a class="header-anchor" href="#deciding-what-to-rename" aria-label="Permalink to “Deciding what to rename”">​</a></h2><p>The rename stage (<code>rename</code>) is plain Rust over the symbol table. It follows Strauss PHP&#39;s order:</p><ol><li>Add the namespaces from each package&#39;s autoload rules.</li><li>Find the symbols that are autoloaded.</li><li>Mark symbols for renaming, minus exclusions: packages, namespaces, files, built-ins and the Wordpress, WooCommerce, WP-CLI and PHP constant presets.</li><li>Work out the new names: the namespace prefix on namespaced symbols, <code>classmap_prefix</code> on global classes, and the function and constant prefixes.</li></ol><p>Symbols PHP provides itself are left out of the table to begin with, so a package that declares a polyfill can&#39;t take over a built-in name.</p><h2 id="making-the-edits" tabindex="-1">Making the edits <a class="header-anchor" href="#making-the-edits" aria-label="Permalink to “Making the edits”">​</a></h2><p>The edit stage turns the plan into a list of <code>Edit { start, end, replacement }</code> values for each file, in terms of byte offsets into the original source. This is what keeps the tool precise:</p><ul><li>All edits refer to the original text, so none moves another, and they can be made in any order.</li><li>Edits that overlap, fall outside the file or land in the middle of a UTF-8 character are an error, and the file is left unchanged.</li><li>A file that isn&#39;t valid UTF-8 is copied as it is, with a warning, since offsets in it can&#39;t be trusted.</li></ul><p>Applying them is a single pass over the original text (<code>edits/apply.rs</code>). Nothing is re-parsed and nothing is reformatted.</p><h3 id="similar" tabindex="-1"><code>similar</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#similar" aria-label="Permalink to “similar”">​</a></h3><p>The <code>diff</code> command applies the edits in memory and shows them as a unified diff with <code>similar</code>, without touching any file. It is the same edit list <code>prefix</code> writes, so what you see is what you get.</p><h2 id="writing-the-result" tabindex="-1">Writing the result <a class="header-anchor" href="#writing-the-result" aria-label="Permalink to “Writing the result”">​</a></h2><p>The output stage copies each file, makes its edits, adds the modification note to the header and writes it, keeping the file&#39;s permissions so an executable stays executable. It then writes the autoloader, applies edits to your own files if <code>update_call_sites</code> is on and finally deletes the originals if you asked for that. Nothing is deleted unless everything before it was written.</p><p>Copying is parallel too. <code>--dry-run</code> goes through the same code with the writes skipped, so its report matches a real run.</p><h3 id="indicatif-and-clap" tabindex="-1"><code>indicatif</code> and <code>clap</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#indicatif-and-clap" aria-label="Permalink to “indicatif and clap”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong><code>indicatif</code></strong> draws the spinners and progress bars. They are drawn only if stderr is a terminal and <code>--quiet</code> isn&#39;t given. When output is piped or logged you get plain lines, and the library code and its tests never draw anything.</li><li><strong><code>clap</code></strong> defines the command line with derive macros (<code>cli/mod.rs</code>), including the <code>--json</code>, <code>--dry-run</code>, <code>--parallel</code> and <code>--quiet</code> options.</li></ul><h2 id="the-crates-at-a-glance" tabindex="-1">The crates at a glance <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-crates-at-a-glance" aria-label="Permalink to “The crates at a glance”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Crate</th><th style="text-align:left;">Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>php-rs-parser</code>, <code>php-ast</code>, <code>php-lexer</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Parsing PHP, resolving names and locating exact positions.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>rayon</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Analysing, listing, planning and writing files in parallel.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>serde</code>, <code>serde_json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Reading configuration and <code>installed.json</code>, and all JSON output.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>ignore</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Walking package directories and your own source.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>globset</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Matching <code>export-ignore</code> patterns from <code>.gitattributes</code>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>fancy-regex</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">PHP-style patterns with lookarounds and backreferences.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>similar</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">The unified diff in <code>diff</code>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>indicatif</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Progress on a terminal.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>clap</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">The command line.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>libc</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Measuring memory in the <code>benchmark</code> command, which is only built with the <code>benchmark</code> feature.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>There is no PHP runtime involved at any point, which is why Strauss-RS is a single binary with nothing else to install.</p>',47)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};